DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       Like nnaannoosslleeeepp(2), cclloocckk__nnaannoosslleeeepp() allows the calling thread to sleep
       for an interval specified with nanosecond precision.  It differs in al‐
       lowing  the caller to select the clock against which the sleep interval
       is to be measured, and in allowing the sleep interval to  be  specified
       as either an absolute or a relative value.

       The time values passed to and returned by this call are specified using
       ttiimmeessppeecc(3) structures.

       The _c_l_o_c_k_i_d argument specifies the clock against which the sleep inter‐
       val  is  to  be  measured.  This argument can have one of the following
       values:

       CCLLOOCCKK__RREEAALLTTIIMMEE
              A settable system‐wide real‐time clock.

       CCLLOOCCKK__TTAAII (since Linux 3.10)
              A system‐wide clock derived from wall‐clock  time  but  counting
              leap seconds.

       CCLLOOCCKK__MMOONNOOTTOONNIICC
              A nonsettable, monotonically increasing clock that measures time
              since  some  unspecified  point in the past that does not change
              after system startup.

       CCLLOOCCKK__BBOOOOTTTTIIMMEE (since Linux 2.6.39)
              Identical to CCLLOOCCKK__MMOONNOOTTOONNIICC, except that it also  includes  any
              time that the system is suspended.

       CCLLOOCCKK__PPRROOCCEESSSS__CCPPUUTTIIMMEE__IIDD
              A  settable per‐process clock that measures CPU time consumed by
              all threads in the process.

       See cclloocckk__ggeettrreess(2) for further details on these clocks.  In  addition,
       the    CPU   clock   IDs   returned   by   cclloocckk__ggeettccppuucclloocckkiidd(3)   and
       pptthhrreeaadd__ggeettccppuucclloocckkiidd(3) can also be passed in _c_l_o_c_k_i_d.

       If _f_l_a_g_s is 0, then the value specified in _t is interpreted as  an  in‐
       terval relative to the current value of the clock specified by _c_l_o_c_k_i_d.

       If _f_l_a_g_s is TTIIMMEERR__AABBSSTTIIMMEE, then _t is interpreted as an absolute time as
       measured by the clock, _c_l_o_c_k_i_d.  If _t is less than or equal to the cur‐
       rent  value  of  the  clock, then cclloocckk__nnaannoosslleeeepp() returns immediately
       without suspending the calling thread.

       cclloocckk__nnaannoosslleeeepp() suspends the execution of the  calling  thread  until
       either at least the time specified by _t has elapsed, or a signal is de‐
       livered  that  causes  a signal handler to be called or that terminates
       the process.

       If the call is interrupted by a signal handler, cclloocckk__nnaannoosslleeeepp() fails
       with the error EEIINNTTRR.  In addition, if _r_e_m_a_i_n is not  NULL,  and  _f_l_a_g_s
       was not TTIIMMEERR__AABBSSTTIIMMEE, it returns the remaining unslept time in _r_e_m_a_i_n.
       This  value  can  then be used to call cclloocckk__nnaannoosslleeeepp() again and com‐
       plete a (relative) sleep.

