NNOOTTEESS
       If  the  interval specified in _t is not an exact multiple of the granu‐
       larity underlying clock  (see  ttiimmee(7)),  then  the  interval  will  be
       rounded  up  to  the  next multiple.  Furthermore, after the sleep com‐
       pletes, there may still be a delay before the CPU becomes free to  once
       again execute the calling thread.

       Using  an  absolute timer is useful for preventing timer drift problems
       of the type described in nnaannoosslleeeepp(2).  (Such problems are  exacerbated
       in programs that try to restart a relative sleep that is repeatedly in‐
       terrupted  by  signals.)  To perform a relative sleep that avoids these
       problems, call cclloocckk__ggeettttiimmee(2) for the desired clock, add the  desired
       interval  to  the  returned time value, and then call cclloocckk__nnaannoosslleeeepp()
       with the TTIIMMEERR__AABBSSTTIIMMEE flag.

       cclloocckk__nnaannoosslleeeepp() is never restarted after being interrupted by a  sig‐
       nal handler, regardless of the use of the ssiiggaaccttiioonn(2) SSAA__RREESSTTAARRTT flag.

       The _r_e_m_a_i_n argument is unused, and unnecessary, when _f_l_a_g_s is TTIIMMEERR__AABB‐‐
       SSTTIIMMEE.  (An absolute sleep can be restarted using the same _t argument.)

       POSIX.1  specifies that cclloocckk__nnaannoosslleeeepp() has no effect on signals dis‐
       positions or the signal mask.

       POSIX.1 specifies that after changing the value of  the  CCLLOOCCKK__RREEAALLTTIIMMEE
       clock via cclloocckk__sseettttiimmee(2), the new clock value shall be used to deter‐
       mine   the   time   at   which   a   thread   blocked  on  an  absolute
       cclloocckk__nnaannoosslleeeepp() will wake up; if the new clock value falls  past  the
       end  of the sleep interval, then the cclloocckk__nnaannoosslleeeepp() call will return
       immediately.

       POSIX.1 specifies that changing the value of the  CCLLOOCCKK__RREEAALLTTIIMMEE  clock
       via  cclloocckk__sseettttiimmee(2)  shall have no effect on a thread that is blocked
       on a relative cclloocckk__nnaannoosslleeeepp().

